WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
The calendars in a planner are grids.

2
00:00:02.481 --> 00:00:06.295
Today we'll build one on an empty
page, one setting at a time,

3
00:00:06.295 --> 00:00:10.112
and look at what each setting
does before adding the next.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.519
Here's the project, made ahead of time with
the generator, which episode 10 covers.

5
00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:20.738
A cover called Q4 2026, three
months under it, and a Notes page.

6
00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:23.580
Every month has a page
for each of its days.

7
00:00:23.798 --> 00:00:26.688
Click a day, and its data
fields show on the right.

8
00:00:26.784 --> 00:00:28.084
Two matter today.

9
00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:32.850
day_num is the date, and weekday_num
is the day of the week as a number:

10
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.643
four, for this Thursday.

11
00:00:34.848 --> 00:00:38.837
The months share a template called
Month, and it's nearly empty:

12
00:00:38.837 --> 00:00:42.328
a title, and a row of weekday
letters, Monday first.

13
00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:45.115
I'll fold Template Settings
away to make room.

14
00:00:45.322 --> 00:00:48.943
G for the grid tool, and drag
out a box under the letters.

15
00:00:49.152 --> 00:00:52.232
A grid draws one cell for
each child of its page.

16
00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:58.342
October's children are its 31 days, so
there are 31 cells, each showing a title.

17
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.500
The Source decides whose
children a grid shows.

18
00:01:01.566 --> 00:01:04.606
The other option pins a
grid to one fixed page.

19
00:01:04.694 --> 00:01:06.844
We'll keep Children of Current Page.

20
00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:09.911
Current means whichever
page is being drawn.

21
00:01:10.047 --> 00:01:13.627
Preview November, and the
grid follows: 30 cells now.

22
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.350
Then back to October.

23
00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:19.564
First, let's make the cells visible.

24
00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:24.067
White cells on white paper are hard
to see, so they get a pale grey fill.

25
00:01:24.162 --> 00:01:27.032
And the black outline round
the whole grid can go.

26
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.680
Cols sets how many cells sit in a row.

27
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:32.257
A week has seven days, so: seven.

28
00:01:32.474 --> 00:01:35.826
They're far too wide, because
W and H, in Geometry,

29
00:01:35.826 --> 00:01:39.034
are the size of one cell,
not the whole grid.

30
00:01:39.091 --> 00:01:41.391
The letters are 60 points apart.

31
00:01:41.441 --> 00:01:45.761
So W is 58, leaving two
for a gap, and H is 60.

32
00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:49.109
Gap X and Gap Y are the
space between cells.

33
00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:50.484
Two points each.

34
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:55.340
Fifty-eight plus two is sixty, and
every column now sits under its letter.

35
00:01:55.545 --> 00:01:59.675
Display Template decides what each
cell says, and it starts as title.

36
00:01:59.721 --> 00:02:04.287
The buttons below insert the days' fields,
but they add to what's in the box,

37
00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:05.901
so I'll clear it first.

38
00:02:06.106 --> 00:02:07.286
Now day_num.

39
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:11.533
Each cell reads it from its
own day page: 01 to 31.

40
00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:16.589
A planner prints these smaller, in the
top-left corner, to leave room for writing.

41
00:02:16.698 --> 00:02:20.738
In Typography: 12 points,
Align Left, and Align Top.

42
00:02:21.184 --> 00:02:23.784
But look where the first is: under Monday.

43
00:02:23.874 --> 00:02:27.274
October the first, 2026, is a Thursday.

44
00:02:27.479 --> 00:02:31.789
Offset (Skip items) leaves blank
cells before the first item.

45
00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:36.009
Three, for Monday to Wednesday,
and October starts on a Thursday.

46
00:02:36.216 --> 00:02:39.446
But a static offset is
the same on every page.

47
00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:40.843
Preview December.

48
00:02:40.876 --> 00:02:44.286
Its first is a Tuesday, yet
it's still under Thursday.

49
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:48.680
Each month needs its own number,
and its first day already knows it.

50
00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:53.743
Switch Static to Dynamic, and the offset
is read from a field on the first item.

51
00:02:53.948 --> 00:02:55.768
The field is weekday_num.

52
00:02:55.832 --> 00:03:00.931
Until the name is complete, nothing moves:
Dynamic falls back to the number in the

53
00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:01.430
box.

54
00:03:01.486 --> 00:03:03.526
Then the first jumps to Wednesday.

55
00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.090
One column out.

56
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:09.479
weekday_num counts from Sunday,
which is zero, so Tuesday is two.

57
00:03:09.574 --> 00:03:14.774
But our columns start with Monday, so every
day sits one column left of its number.

58
00:03:14.981 --> 00:03:18.731
The plus-minus box adds to
the field's value: minus one.

59
00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:22.951
Two minus one is one blank cell,
and the first is under Tuesday.

60
00:03:23.163 --> 00:03:25.133
Same settings, every month.

61
00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:29.937
Back to October: four minus one is
three, and it's under Thursday again.

62
00:03:30.141 --> 00:03:32.221
November is the one to watch.

63
00:03:32.284 --> 00:03:37.404
It starts on a Sunday, weekday zero,
and zero minus one is minus one.

64
00:03:37.612 --> 00:03:41.964
There's no column minus one, so a
negative offset wraps round once:

65
00:03:41.964 --> 00:03:44.552
add the seven columns, and it's six.

66
00:03:44.644 --> 00:03:48.724
The first lands under Sunday,
and the 30th starts a sixth row.

67
00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.198
On to the cover.

68
00:03:50.228 --> 00:03:53.337
I'd like the three months
there, in a row, as links.

69
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.770
G again, and a strip under the title.

70
00:03:56.761 --> 00:03:59.955
One cell per child again:
three months, and Notes.

71
00:04:00.027 --> 00:04:03.286
Final Data Subset takes
a window of that list.

72
00:04:03.382 --> 00:04:06.522
Start skips from the front,
and Count keeps that many.

73
00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:08.977
Count three, and Notes drops out.

74
00:04:09.288 --> 00:04:11.158
Now a calendar for the cover.

75
00:04:11.274 --> 00:04:14.095
Rather than build it
again, I'll copy ours.

76
00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:19.069
On Month, V, drag a box across the
letters and the calendar, and Control C.

77
00:04:19.432 --> 00:04:21.362
On the cover, Control V.

78
00:04:21.406 --> 00:04:24.404
Pasted things land 20 points
down and to the right,

79
00:04:24.404 --> 00:04:26.712
so I'll drag them under the months.

80
00:04:27.657 --> 00:04:32.536
The copy reads the cover's children:
three months and Notes, so four cells.

81
00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:36.670
A month has no day_num of its own,
so it borrows its first day's.

82
00:04:36.874 --> 00:04:40.924
Deep Traversal walks further down
the tree, one level at a time.

83
00:04:41.005 --> 00:04:44.821
Add Level, and give it a Count
of one: of the cover's children,

84
00:04:44.821 --> 00:04:46.525
take the first, October.

85
00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:51.120
Add Level again, and leave its Count
empty: every child of October.

86
00:04:51.239 --> 00:04:53.639
The days are back, starting on Thursday.

87
00:04:53.844 --> 00:04:57.244
Level one's Start picks the
month, counting from zero.

88
00:04:57.363 --> 00:05:01.837
One is November, wrapped round to
Sunday as it was on its own page.

89
00:05:02.054 --> 00:05:05.264
Start three is Notes, which
has no pages under it.

90
00:05:05.396 --> 00:05:09.888
With nothing to show, the editor fills
the grid with Item placeholders,

91
00:05:09.888 --> 00:05:12.226
and the PDF prints nothing there.

92
00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.440
Back to one.

93
00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.290
Grid Configuration lists its
steps in the order they run.

94
00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:20.919
The source first, then Deep Traversal.

95
00:05:20.957 --> 00:05:24.151
Then Final Data Subset,
and the offset last.

96
00:05:24.281 --> 00:05:28.781
Episode 19 uses that order to
fix grids that look wrong.

97
00:05:28.987 --> 00:05:33.137
Every cell is a link to the page
it shows, with nothing to set up.

98
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:37.080
The canvas doesn't follow links,
so let's export and try them.

99
00:05:38.503 --> 00:05:43.463
December, in the month row, opens
December's page, starting on a Tuesday.

100
00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:46.119
And every day opens its own page.

101
00:05:46.155 --> 00:05:47.995
Christmas Day is a Friday.

102
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.080
That's a grid built up
from one cell per child.

103
00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:55.899
Next time: references, and how one
page can appear in two places at once.
